"\section=1"
"\name=companion"
"\summary=start Steam client with Companion module"

namespace companion
{
  {
    "<options> <steam-arg>",

    "\h|SYNOPSIS|

     \c{\b{companion --help}\n
        \b{companion --version}\n
        \b{companion --steam} [\b{--} <steam-arg>...]}

     \h|DESCRIPTION|

     The \cb{companion} program starts the native Linux Steam client with the
     Companion module preloaded. While the module is loaded, Steam friends
     see IW4x running under Proton as the game being played. The arguments
     after \cb{--} are passed to the Steam command unchanged, for example:

     \
     $ companion --steam -- -silent
     \

     The Steam client only loads the module at startup. If the client is
     already running without Companion, then \cb{companion} asks it to exit
     with \cb{steam -shutdown}, waits for it to exit, and starts it again
     with the module. Note that \cb{companion} never kills the client and
     fails if it doesn't exit within 120 seconds. If Companion is already
     active in the running client, then \cb{companion} executes the Steam
     command without the module, which passes the arguments to the running
     client.

     The module is preloaded from the \cb{lib/companion/} directory of the
     installation that \cb{companion} belongs to, specifically, from its
     subdirectory named after the dynamic linker's \cb{$PLATFORM} token (see
     \cb{ld.so(8)}). In the 32-bit Steam client this token expands to
     \cb{i686}, which contains the module. In the 64-bit programs that start
     the client it expands to \cb{x86_64}, \cb{haswell}, or \cb{xeon_phi},
     each of which contains an empty 64-bit stand-in that keeps the dynamic
     linker of these programs from warning about the preload. Note that the
     module removes itself from \cb{LD_PRELOAD} as it loads, so the processes
     that the client starts, such as Proton and the game, don't inherit it.

     The dynamic linker splits \cb{LD_PRELOAD} at spaces and colons and
     provides no way to escape them. If the module directory contains either,
     which is normally the case when it is inside the game installation, then
     \cb{companion} preloads the module through the \cb{companion} symbolic
     link to this directory that it creates in \cb{$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}.

     Companion writes its diagnostics to the Steam client's \cb{stderr},
     which the client normally copies to its console log
     (\cb{~/.steam/steam/logs/console-linux.txt}). Each line starts with
     \cb{[companion]} and a successful startup ends with a line similar to
     the following:

     \
     [companion] active (build 2)
     \

     See \l{libcompanion-manual The Companion Manual} for details on how
     Companion works and on the other diagnostics that it issues."
  }

  class options
  {
    "\h|OPTIONS|"

    bool --steam
    {
      "Start the Steam client with the Companion module preloaded by
       executing the Steam command in place of \cb{companion}."
    }

    bool --help;
    bool --version;
  };

  {
    "",

    "\h|ENVIRONMENT|

     The \cb{COMPANION_STEAM} environment variable specifies the Steam
     command to execute. If it is unset or empty, then \cb{steam} is searched
     for in \cb{PATH}.

     The module is added in front of the existing \cb{LD_PRELOAD} value,
     which is otherwise preserved.

     The \cb{XDG_RUNTIME_DIR} environment variable specifies the directory
     of the symbolic link to the module directory (see above). It is only
     used if the module directory contains spaces or colons.

     \h|FILES|

     \dl|

     \li|\c{\i{prefix}\b{/lib/companion/i686/libcompanion.so}}

     Companion module.|

     \li|\c{\i{prefix}\b{/lib/companion/x86_64/libcompanion.so}}

     Empty 64-bit stand-in. The \cb{haswell/} and \cb{xeon_phi/}
     subdirectories contain the same stand-in.|

     \li|\c{\b{/dev/shm/companion.}\i{uid}}

     Companion status, which the game reads. Companion keeps this file
     locked while it runs, which allows only one active Companion per
     user.||

     \h|EXIT STATUS|

     Non-zero exit status is returned in case of an error, for example, if
     the module is not installed or the running Steam client doesn't exit.
     Otherwise, with \cb{--steam}, \cb{companion} executes the Steam command
     in its place and the exit status is that of the Steam command."
  }
}
